[{:db/id 17592186119962 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e14b31da-25df-5fa5-9bfc-4a5fa3c83d9a" "question-d671cb3b-5175-53db-90fa-e1b9c7534825" "question-d642175b-da68-5c1f-a969-cd59bcc47e2b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a4a1842a-2984-5d5e-9da6-07b14124ecae" "question-b46ee022-1c34-5022-81e0-9eb66a5f128b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119543 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 5\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 5I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\sin(3t) = 5I + \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I = 5\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(3t) + \\beta\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +5\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{15}{34},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{25}{34}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{15}{34}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{25}{34}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.86\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e14b31da-25df-5fa5-9bfc-4a5fa3c83d9a" :question/id #uuid "e14b31da-25df-5fa5-9bfc-4a5fa3c83d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336528" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds)." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=40I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-35285a5f-8b43-594b-bed5-a8b1f342b566"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35285a5f-8b43-594b-bed5-a8b1f342b566" :answer-field/id #uuid "35285a5f-8b43-594b-bed5-a8b1f342b566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8f65e13e-8ff1-5838-8e62-92f884f5c545" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f65e13e-8ff1-5838-8e62-92f884f5c545" "answer-7814a7c6-4a23-5616-8c4b-d78da059e449" "answer-d31d4135-c6f8-5435-93db-b00252a59a39" "answer-15765ceb-c21b-5d75-8bab-59640665051b" "answer-4fe27691-a46e-52d0-ab71-166079f3d08f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f65e13e-8ff1-5838-8e62-92f884f5c545" :answer/id #uuid "8f65e13e-8ff1-5838-8e62-92f884f5c545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7814a7c6-4a23-5616-8c4b-d78da059e449" :answer/id #uuid "7814a7c6-4a23-5616-8c4b-d78da059e449" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+40I=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d31d4135-c6f8-5435-93db-b00252a59a39" :answer/id #uuid "d31d4135-c6f8-5435-93db-b00252a59a39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15765ceb-c21b-5d75-8bab-59640665051b" :answer/id #uuid "15765ceb-c21b-5d75-8bab-59640665051b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-10I=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fe27691-a46e-52d0-ab71-166079f3d08f" :answer/id #uuid "4fe27691-a46e-52d0-ab71-166079f3d08f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-40I=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d671cb3b-5175-53db-90fa-e1b9c7534825" :question/id #uuid "d671cb3b-5175-53db-90fa-e1b9c7534825" :question/math-academy-id "q-336549" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a84a1501854f6efb988b7c02c3a1b5a80b80dbf3d5fc7ce94406778cc6dcadb5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $10\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=10I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be5ee839-525c-52d1-82fe-55826f7f18d3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be5ee839-525c-52d1-82fe-55826f7f18d3" :answer-field/id #uuid "be5ee839-525c-52d1-82fe-55826f7f18d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6905d1fa-aede-5dec-8b9c-01fc827b99e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3085092-7447-53c2-ab73-8ce83fc8ca20" "answer-6f549ead-c18c-53dc-9b35-4c7cf5360aa5" "answer-6905d1fa-aede-5dec-8b9c-01fc827b99e6" "answer-c66c7a80-1279-5afd-8698-02299e425280" "answer-e82fada6-004d-5f78-9c37-7f317f030764"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3085092-7447-53c2-ab73-8ce83fc8ca20" :answer/id #uuid "c3085092-7447-53c2-ab73-8ce83fc8ca20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f549ead-c18c-53dc-9b35-4c7cf5360aa5" :answer/id #uuid "6f549ead-c18c-53dc-9b35-4c7cf5360aa5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6905d1fa-aede-5dec-8b9c-01fc827b99e6" :answer/id #uuid "6905d1fa-aede-5dec-8b9c-01fc827b99e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c66c7a80-1279-5afd-8698-02299e425280" :answer/id #uuid "c66c7a80-1279-5afd-8698-02299e425280" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-10I=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e82fada6-004d-5f78-9c37-7f317f030764" :answer/id #uuid "e82fada6-004d-5f78-9c37-7f317f030764" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d642175b-da68-5c1f-a969-cd59bcc47e2b" :question/id #uuid "d642175b-da68-5c1f-a969-cd59bcc47e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336544" :question/problem "![](images/da/da02d808aac69b1b7381f6e6ca7a006b863e6b808a89ecc7a4f79be0db7556b9.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $24\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=24I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fec331a1-3371-57b3-ae6b-a96dcb6d5ba8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fec331a1-3371-57b3-ae6b-a96dcb6d5ba8" :answer-field/id #uuid "fec331a1-3371-57b3-ae6b-a96dcb6d5ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-996409b0-e6fa-57e7-99b9-cd8b61cd400f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f76c4adb-4da0-59aa-acf5-290d24892ca1" "answer-afc3c83d-c96b-579a-8e82-93fce2051362" "answer-996409b0-e6fa-57e7-99b9-cd8b61cd400f" "answer-6c963e0a-5b77-567a-8321-54badb205db6" "answer-4b64b87f-eca7-5f41-834e-0c109a362f08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f76c4adb-4da0-59aa-acf5-290d24892ca1" :answer/id #uuid "f76c4adb-4da0-59aa-acf5-290d24892ca1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afc3c83d-c96b-579a-8e82-93fce2051362" :answer/id #uuid "afc3c83d-c96b-579a-8e82-93fce2051362" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+3I=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-996409b0-e6fa-57e7-99b9-cd8b61cd400f" :answer/id #uuid "996409b0-e6fa-57e7-99b9-cd8b61cd400f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c963e0a-5b77-567a-8321-54badb205db6" :answer/id #uuid "6c963e0a-5b77-567a-8321-54badb205db6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-4I=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b64b87f-eca7-5f41-834e-0c109a362f08" :answer/id #uuid "4b64b87f-eca7-5f41-834e-0c109a362f08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dI}{dt}-6I=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311391 :answer-field/correct "answer-35ebd7fe-0ae4-53ef-a9ea-c17e7e684c54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35ebd7fe-0ae4-53ef-a9ea-c17e7e684c54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35ebd7fe-0ae4-53ef-a9ea-c17e7e684c54" :answer/id #uuid "35ebd7fe-0ae4-53ef-a9ea-c17e7e684c54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186377220 :answer-field/correct "answer-ed100424-66cf-5f20-9b03-93d588806dce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed100424-66cf-5f20-9b03-93d588806dce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed100424-66cf-5f20-9b03-93d588806dce" :answer/id #uuid "ed100424-66cf-5f20-9b03-93d588806dce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186377223 :answer-field/correct "answer-201094b7-9ea7-547e-8ae2-50c5f5152016" :answer-field/choices ["answer-201094b7-9ea7-547e-8ae2-50c5f5152016"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-201094b7-9ea7-547e-8ae2-50c5f5152016" :answer/id #uuid "201094b7-9ea7-547e-8ae2-50c5f5152016" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}e^{-10t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120259 :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.01$ seconds given that the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.01$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5=20I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+10{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =\\frac{5}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-10t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.01$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.01)=\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-10(0.01)}+1)≈1.83\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117703 :answer-field/correct "answer-aedf5696-b202-5101-a939-41ba9ccd7759" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aedf5696-b202-5101-a939-41ba9ccd7759"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aedf5696-b202-5101-a939-41ba9ccd7759" :answer/id #uuid "aedf5696-b202-5101-a939-41ba9ccd7759" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.83" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186377270 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(6\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +6\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 6\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{3},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}≈1.49\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a4a1842a-2984-5d5e-9da6-07b14124ecae" :question/id #uuid "a4a1842a-2984-5d5e-9da6-07b14124ecae" :question/math-academy-id "q-336816" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cc4feb2e391a0a25bb531592205dbc738f0fd0582fb2d56a1627a8584a42618.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{12}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{17})}^{2}+{(\\frac{12}{17})}^{2}}≈0.73\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c3b11f6-8e7a-5eeb-8b4a-ea3e87e2cec9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c3b11f6-8e7a-5eeb-8b4a-ea3e87e2cec9" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c3b11f6-8e7a-5eeb-8b4a-ea3e87e2cec9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-17fb0566-042b-5e63-b5fb-b9534f0e3280" :answer-field/choices ["answer-17fb0566-042b-5e63-b5fb-b9534f0e3280"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17fb0566-042b-5e63-b5fb-b9534f0e3280" :answer/id #uuid "17fb0566-042b-5e63-b5fb-b9534f0e3280" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.73" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186377273 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $4\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=4I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{4}{17})}^{2}+{(\\frac{16}{17})}^{2}}≈0.97\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b46ee022-1c34-5022-81e0-9eb66a5f128b" :question/id #uuid "b46ee022-1c34-5022-81e0-9eb66a5f128b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336658" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e329b7c1fdb9652affd6381c5002efe4c8eed6fa2b13da43c9a2a6116daa386b.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=5I+1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{12}{29},$ $\\beta =\\frac{30}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{12}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{30}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{12}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{30}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{12}{29},\\,\\beta =\\frac{30}{29}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{12}{29}+\\frac{30}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2eefd91d-419f-55e9-93a6-15c6bdbecaa5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2eefd91d-419f-55e9-93a6-15c6bdbecaa5" :answer-field/id #uuid "2eefd91d-419f-55e9-93a6-15c6bdbecaa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-870c4e55-f399-5e97-a85e-6ca59c9165d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a4bad54-9152-542d-909c-398b06293aaa" "answer-25bc7e77-a1ba-503a-9c91-af56d4db8aba" "answer-870c4e55-f399-5e97-a85e-6ca59c9165d1" "answer-69b63830-b726-5d99-92b7-09497a69031c" "answer-bdca223a-55f2-5ca4-98ab-6d1397c5cd69"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a4bad54-9152-542d-909c-398b06293aaa" :answer/id #uuid "4a4bad54-9152-542d-909c-398b06293aaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14}{29}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25bc7e77-a1ba-503a-9c91-af56d4db8aba" :answer/id #uuid "25bc7e77-a1ba-503a-9c91-af56d4db8aba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{29}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-870c4e55-f399-5e97-a85e-6ca59c9165d1" :answer/id #uuid "870c4e55-f399-5e97-a85e-6ca59c9165d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{18}{29}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69b63830-b726-5d99-92b7-09497a69031c" :answer/id #uuid "69b63830-b726-5d99-92b7-09497a69031c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{20}{29}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdca223a-55f2-5ca4-98ab-6d1397c5cd69" :answer/id #uuid "bdca223a-55f2-5ca4-98ab-6d1397c5cd69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{22}{29}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311744 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=12I+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{8}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{2}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{17},\\,\\beta =\\frac{8}{17}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{2}{17}+\\frac{8}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120983 :tutorial/content "In many cases, the EMF produced by a power source is sinusoidal, not constant. Such a power source is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/43/4398148f91eda2bae1392f79e868e10c8967ecc23ca9f26b791bc1fbacc45ff9.png)\n\nIn a first-order linear RL circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient current that decays over time and a steady-state current that oscillates with the same frequency as the input and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nFor example, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state current in the RL circuit shown below.\n\n![](images/59/59fd8a8db1a227a578670d2457011275446c87ba218686f8d1984f4a73a8cfa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 7\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\sin(4t) = 7I + 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I = 3\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(7\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +7\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{12}{65},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{21}{65}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-7t} - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-7t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) =  - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t) \\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{12}{65}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{21}{65}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.37\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. This amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the steady-state current flowing through the circuit." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120008 :activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
